// T4 string release, the POSITIVE case: a string local's data buffer is
// reclaimed.
//
// A string is a FAT VALUE -- `{ i8*, i64 }`, held inline (type_mapping.c). The
// heap object is field 0, the data buffer, so the release targets the field and
// not the slot. That is the same shape the slice probe covers; the ELEMENT COUNT
// is what tells the two apart, 2 here against a slice's 3.
//
// THE BINDING SITE IS A CONCATENATION, and it is the reason this probe leaks
// anything at all. `s := a + b` lowers to goo_string_concat, which ALWAYS
// returns fresh goo_alloc memory and COPIES both operands, so the local is the
// one owner. Before the AST_BINARY_EXPR arm in release_decision.c that binding
// read RELEASE_NO_NOT_OWNED, and the only owned string bindings were a Goo call
// and a non-retaining shim -- neither of which ordinary code writes to build a
// string.
//
// `a` IS NOT RELEASED, and that is not a hole. A literal carries a real header
// whose count is GOO_RC_IMMORTAL (scripts/string_literal_header_probe.sh), so it
// owns no heap object and condition 2 refuses it. A release on one is a no-op
// rather than free() on .rodata.
//
// Called in a loop, so the difference is measurable rather than theoretical.
// Without a release this leaks one 16-byte GOO_OBJ_HEADER plus a 33-byte payload
// per iteration.
//
// IMPORT-FREE, for the reason the other arc probes record: an unresolved import
// makes a confident table read as conservative, and a gate whose verdict can
// move for an unrelated cause is not a gate.
package main

var sink int

func work(n int) {
	a := "abcdefghijklmnop"
	s := a + "qrstuvwxyz012345"
	sink = sink + len(s) + n
}

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		work(i)
	}
}
